othman seeks another term in a Wisconsin district that leans Republican by about eight points. He enters the general election with a large financial advantage, having raised roughly $929,000 to Democrat Brad Smith's $166,000, and holds a strong cash-on-hand edge. Smith won his primary in August and earned an AFL-CIO endorsement, but independent and Green candidates on the ballot are unlikely to alter the race's structure.\n\nEvery major expert rating, from Cook to Sabato to the Economist, places the seat at Safe or Solid Republican, and prediction markets price Grothman's chances around 94 percent. The only available poll, a Democratic-sponsored May survey of likely voters, showed Grothman ahead 61 to 39. The forecast expects a comfortable Republican win in the low double digits.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.0556, "margin": -10.42}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.0454, "margin": -12.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.0701, "margin": -11.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.063, "margin": -12.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.0353, "margin": -12.0}]}